<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=chain-pump">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chain pump</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Dragon backbone, Square-pallet chain pump</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A chain pump is a water pump in which circular discs or square pallets are fixed to an endless chain. One part of the chain dips into the water and runs up a tube; water trapped between the discs is lifted and discharged at the top. The earliest evidence is a Babylonian text from about 700 BCE. Versions appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from about 200 BCE, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from at least the 1st century CE, where Wang Chong described one around 80 CE. Chinese pumps were called dragon backbones and spread through the countryside, mostly for irrigation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chain-pump.jpg" alt="Chain pump" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maproom · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rope_pump_in_Museo_Nazionale_Scienza_e_Tecnologia_Leonardo_da_Vinci.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Farming changed fast: early-ripening Champa rice allowed two crops a year in the south, dams and <b>chain pumps</b> opened new fields, and families made wine, charcoal, paper and silk to sell at local markets.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
